WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:05.500
Venom first sank his teeth into fans' brains
decades ago in the pages of The Amazing Spider-Man

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:09.420
comics, giving the antihero a long time to
build up a cast of kooky side characters.

00:00:09.929 --> 00:00:14.660
Not surprisingly, a handful of those familiar
names made their way into 2018's Venom, the

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:16.820
symbiote's first solo big screen outing.

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:20.920
Here are some of the characters in Venom who
have way more meaning than you realized.

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:22.620
Major spoilers ahead.

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:24.200
Anne Weying

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:29.750
In Venom, Michelle Williams plays Anne Weying,
Eddie Brock's fiancee - who dumps him after

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:33.940
he snuck information about The Life Foundation
out of her personal files and then accosted

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:36.660
the foundation's CEO, Carlton Drake, in an
interview.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:40.500
The move gets both of them fired, and Weying
breaks off their engagement.

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:45.040
She later gets catapulted back into his life
when Venom takes over her body so that she

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:46.420
can carry him back to Eddie.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:51.280
For a few minutes, audiences were treated
to a giant, head-eating female Venom.

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:56.080
That bizarre version of Venom comes straight
out of the comic book miniseries Sinner Takes

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:56.580
All.

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:00.400
In that arc, Venom bonds with Anne to save
her life after Anne gets shot.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:04.500
Later in the series, they pull the same trick
to get out of a hostage situation, saving

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:07.160
the day by way of perfectly planned bodyswapping.

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:09.080
Carlton Drake

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:14.560
2018's Venom saw Rhiz Amed channeling the
prototypical bad guy in his character, Carlton

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:15.080
Drake.

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:18.880
He's an amoral scumbag who eventually gets
a symbiote of his very own.

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:22.700
He and Venom punch each other a lot, and then
one of them explodes.

00:01:22.910 --> 00:01:25.320
In the comics, however, there's a bit more
to this guy's story.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:29.800
Like many of Venom's adversaries, Carlton
Drake was originally a Spider-Man villain.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:35.060
He and his nefarious Life Foundation first
showed up in The Amazing Spider-Man #298,

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:39.800
and it only took him a few issues to throw
an army of mutated monsters at Spidey.

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:43.750
His big clash with Venom came in Venom: Lethal
Protector, which also served as one of the

00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:46.240
storylines Sony's Venom film was based on.

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:51.160
In that series, Drake concocts a plan to steal
five pieces of Venom and nurture them into

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:53.180
his own symbiote super soldiers.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:58.180
He succeeds, creating Scream, Phage, Lasher,
Agony, and Riot.

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:02.560
Eventually, Spider-Man and Venom put a stop
to this particular plan, sending Drake back

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:07.250
into hiding to concoct bigger, more devious
schemes which culminate in Drake turning himself

00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:10.180
into a giant spider in the Arachnis Project
story arc.

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:12.120
Roland Treece

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:16.600
He didn't have a lot going on in the movie,
but Scott Haze delivered a pretty good henchman

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:17.600
as Roland Treece.

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:21.320
Sure, he got his head eaten in the end, but
it's the journey that counts.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:25.040
Unfortunately, in this case, that journey
should have been a whole lot bigger.

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:29.880
In his comics incarnation, Treece isn't a
low-level thug who goes out to crummy apartments

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:34.370
to retrieve his boss's lost aliens - he's
an ultra-rich businessman who sits on the

00:02:34.370 --> 00:02:36.320
board of directors for The Life Foundation.

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:40.440
Granted, he's still less of a threat to Venom
than Drake, but it's Treece's master plan

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:44.150
to demolish part of the city that sends Spidey
and Venom running back to San Francisco for

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:46.200
the final showdown in Lethal Protector.

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:47.420
And that's all fine.

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:51.960
The Venom movie didn't need multiple masterminds
for its crazy schemes, and there were already

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:53.420
enough bad guys to go around.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.420
At least the movie didn't try to shoehorn
in Venomsaurus Rex.

00:02:58.240 --> 00:02:59.500
Wait, who are we kidding?

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:02.920
Venomsaurus Rex would have been the best addition
to this movie.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:04.340
Scream

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:09.640
In the first act of the movie, we're introduced
to some tanks of goo in the Life Foundation

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:10.140
labs.

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:14.980
Those are the symbiotes that Drake is trying
to merge with human hosts - a project that

00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:16.680
goes disastrously wrong.

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:20.610
If you paid close attention, you may have
noticed that one of the symbiotes was distinctly

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:25.340
yellow, which matches another distinctly yellow
symbiote in the comics: Scream.

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:30.260
In Venom: Lethal Protector, Donna Diego was
the human host for Scream, one of the five

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:31.900
symbiotes created by Drake.

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:35.640
She eventually obtained a measure of self
awareness, and gained enough control over

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:37.780
her symbiote to start hunting the other ones.

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:43.320
Fans on the internet speculated that the EMT
who bonds with a symbiote was, in fact, Donna

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:43.940
Diego.

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:48.860
Although we now know that that lady was just
a brief host for the Riot symbiote, her inclusion

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:53.240
- and the fact that she got more screentime
than the other hosts - could be another nod

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:54.220
to Donna Diego.

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:55.100
Maybe.

00:03:55.820 --> 00:03:56.920
Cletus Kasady

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:02.000
Before the reviews were even in, Venom was
already setting up a sequel in its Spiderless

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:02.580
Spiderverse.

00:04:03.130 --> 00:04:07.500
In the mid-credits scene, we see Woody Harrelson
make an appearance as a red-haired psychopath

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:10.380
in a prison cell who goes by the name Cletus
Kasady.

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:14.640
Comics fans should instantly recognize the
name as the inmate who played host to Venom's

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:15.580
son, Carnage.

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:17.380
"Kasady is gone.

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:19.060
There is only Carnage!"

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:25.360
He may be Venom's son, but Carnage is stronger
and and a whole lot crazier than dear old

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:25.860
dad.

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:30.320
If a Venom sequel gets off the ground, a Woody
Harrelson-played Carnage could be just the

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:32.130
ticket to take things to the next level.

